@@ -67,6 +67,31 @@ After an update, run `fwctl --device sensor-0042 history` to review the outcome
 recorded locally, or `fwctl doctor` if a device does not show up in `fwctl
 devices`.
 
+## Staged updates
+
+A transfer over serial takes minutes, while the window in which a reboot is safe
+may only open hours later. `stage` and `activate` split `update` in two so field
+crews can separate the two costs:
+
+```sh
+fwctl --device sensor-0042 stage sensor-node-1.2.0.fwpkg
+fwctl staged
+# hours later, once a reboot is safe
+fwctl --device sensor-0042 activate
+```
+
+`stage` does everything `update` does up to and including device-side digest
+verification. The candidate slot is never selected and the device is never
+rebooted, so it carries on running confirmed firmware. Anything that stops
+staging short, from policy rejection to a bad digest, stages nothing.
+
+Each staged run writes one record per device to `staging_file`, newest replacing
+older. `activate` finishes from that record alone and consumes it whatever the
+outcome. If the device no longer matches the record on product, running version,
+rollback counter, held transfer, or accepted bytes, activation stops with a
+`staged_update_lost` error before boot state moves; activating a device with no
+record reports `nothing_staged`.
+
 Every command accepts `--json`; progress remains on standard error so standard
 output is a single machine-readable record. `--quiet` suppresses successful human
 output, while `--verbose` reports configuration and connection detail.
